.\par 12.\emdash  I am placed, you are placed, to be placed \emdash  I have been placed.\par \par NOTES\par }{\b\f210 1. Pot}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 re}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 to drink}{\f208  (}{\b\f210 p}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 to}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 p}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 tas}{\f208 , etc.); }{\b\f210 p}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 tio}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 the drink}{\f208  (subject); which, if it is a direct object, becomes: }{\b\f210 poti}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 nem}{\f208 . }{\b\f210 P}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 culum}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 the cup}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 the beaker}{\f208 , forms also part of the same family.\par }{\b\f210 2. Interrog}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 tur}{\f208 : passive voice; in the active voice, we would have: }{\b\f210 int}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 rrogat}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 he questions}{\f208 ; similarly }{\b\f210 p}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 nitur}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 he is placed}{\f208  and }{\b\f210 p}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 nit}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 he places}{\f208 . You will compare phrase 2 of the exercise with phrase 1 of the lesson.\par }{\b\f210 3. A diurn}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 rio}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 ab }{\f208 a}{\b\f210 vo m}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 o}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 s}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 ne }{\f208 u}{\b\f210 llo d}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 bio}{\f208 : all these forms in }{\b\f210 o}{\f208  correspond to adverbial phrases. Note that the preposition }{\b\f210 a}{\f208  becomes }{\b\f210 ab}{\f208  in front of a vowel, similarly }{\b\f210 e}{\f208 , which indicates extraction, becomes }{\b\f210 ex}{\f208  in front of a vowel.\par }{\b\f210 4. C}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 no}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I sing}{\f208 , or }{\i\f211 I play an instrument}{\f208 ; infinitive: }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 nere}{\f208 ; present indicative: }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 no}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 nis}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 nit}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 nimus}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 nitis}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 nunt}{\f208 . }{\b\f210 Can}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 bat}{\f208  is an imperfect tense.\par }{\b\f210 5. Ch}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 rdae m}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 lleis percuti}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 ntur}{\f208  (passive). By changing the verb to the active voice, the sentence transforms into: }{\b\f210 m}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 llei ch}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 rdas perc}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 tiunt}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 the hammers strike the strings}{\f208 . If we put all the words into the singular, we shall have: }{\b\f210 ch}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 rda m}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 lleo perc}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 titur}{\f208  and }{\b\f210 m}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 lleus ch}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 rdam p}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 rcutit}{\f208 . Do not seek to memorize all of these variations yet: just know that they exist.\par }{\b\f210 6. }{\f208 The passive verbs and those which are conjugated in the passive form, form their perfect tense by means of the perfect participle and of the verb }{\b\f210 sum}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I am}{\f208 . It is very easy, but you will not have to forget after that that the participle agrees in the same way as an adjective. A woman will say therefore: }{\b\f210 p}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 sita sum}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I was placed}{\f208 .\par \par }{\i\f211 The excerpts from magazines and from books which we shall give you from time to time sometimes depart from the original text. You will find further references on the works cited in the }{\scaps\f208 Bibliography}{\i\f211  which is found at the end of the volume.\par }{\f208 \par EXERCITATIO\par \par 1.\emdash  The musician is not hungry, but wants to drink [something] (}{\i\f211 a-drink}{\f208 ).\par 2.\emdash  The journalist interviews (}{\i\f211 questions}{\f208 ) the boozy musician.\par 3.\emdash  The musician puts the violin(}{\i\f211 s}{\f208 ) in the car.\par 4.\emdash  The violin(}{\i\f211 s}{\f208 ) [is] (}{\i\f211 are}{\f208 ) [placed] (}{\i\f211 put}{\f208 ) in the car.\par 5.\emdash  The violin(}{\i\f211 s}{\f208 ) [has] (}{\i\f211 have}{\f208 ) been/[was] (}{\i\f211 were}{\f208 ) placed in the car.\par 6.\emdash  The suitcase has been / was put in the car.\par 7.\emdash  [It has been / was difficult to put] the piano in the small room (}{\i\f211 with-difficulty has been / was put}{\f208 ).\par 8.\emdash  How much does (}{\i\f211 do}{\f208 ) the violin(}{\i\f211 s}{\f208 ) cost? [It] (}{\i\f211 they}{\f208 ) costs (}{\i\f211 cost}{\f208 ) a thousand francs.\par 9.\emdash  Why did you choose room 23? Because from the window I can see the port.\par \par }{\i\f211 Have you noticed that phrases }{\b\i\f212 5}{\i\f211 , }{\b\i\f212 6}{\i\f211  and }{\b\i\f212 7}{\i\f211  of the exercise are examples of what is pointed out in note }{\b\i\f212 6}{\i\f211 ? You can at the same time deduce from that that }{\b\i\f212 fid}{\i\f211 i}{\b\i\f212 culae}{\i\f211  is feminine (plural), }{\b\i\f212 b}{\i\f211 u}{\b\i\f212 lga}{\i\f211 , feminine, and }{\b\i\f212 clavich}{\i\f211 o}{\b\i\f212 rdium}{\i\f211 , neuter.}{\par }}